  "title": "Sanok — the Gateway to the Bieszczady",
  "lead": "Sanok is the gateway to the Bieszczady and one of the oldest cities in Poland. Open-air museum, icons, castle. Slow travel through the Subcarpathian region.",
  "body": "<h2>Why it's worth it</h2><p>Sanok is a city in the Subcarpathian voivodeship, on the San river, at the foot of the Bieszczady. It is one of the oldest cities in Poland — it received city rights in 1339, but settlement here dates back to the early Middle Ages. Sanok is a city that has everything a city on the border of cultures should have: a castle, an open-air museum, Orthodox churches, Roman Catholic churches, a synagogue, and museums. Add to that unique cultural heritage: the Historical Museum in Sanok with the largest collection of icons in Poland, the Museum of Folk Architecture (open-air museum), and works by Zdzisław Beksiński — one of the most outstanding Polish contemporary painters.</p><p>Slow travel through Sanok is a journey through a landscape that surprises with its diversity. The city has a castle, an open-air museum, museums, and Orthodox churches — all within a few kilometres. It is worth spending 2–3 days here to truly feel this landscape. The best way to explore Sanok is on foot, and the Bieszczady by car — public transport from Rzeszów and Kraków is good (trains, buses).</p><h2>What to see</h2><p>Sanok is divided into three main parts: the centre (Market Square and castle), the open-air museum (Museum of Folk Architecture), and the Bieszczady (surroundings). Exploring the city and surroundings takes 2–3 days.</p><h3>Centre</h3><ul><li><strong>Market Square</strong> — the main square of the city, with townhouses and a town hall. Worth it for architecture.</li><li><strong>Royal Castle</strong> — a 14th-century castle, now home to the Historical Museum. Worth it for architecture.</li><li><strong>Historical Museum</strong> — a museum with the largest collection of icons in Poland (about 700). Worth it for culture.</li></ul><h3>Open-air museum</h3><ul><li><strong>Museum of Folk Architecture</strong> — one of the largest open-air museums in Poland, with buildings of the Pogórzanie, Lemkos, and Boykos. Worth it for culture.</li><li><strong>Open-air Orthodox churches</strong> — several Orthodox churches moved to the museum from the area. Worth it for architecture.</li><li><strong>Farmsteads</strong> — numerous peasant farmsteads from the 19th and 20th centuries. Worth it for culture.</li></ul><h3>Bieszczady</h3><ul><li><strong>Bieszczady</strong> — mountains south of Sanok, among the wildest in Poland. Worth it for hiking.</li><li><strong>Solina</strong> — a reservoir lake in the Bieszczady, with numerous harbours. Worth it for sailing.</li><li><strong>Lesko</strong> — a small town with a synagogue and castle, on the way to the Bieszczady. Worth it for architecture.</li></ul><h2>When to go</h2><ul><li><strong>May and June</strong> — the best time for slow travel. Warm, green, few tourists.</li><li><strong>July–August</strong> — Bieszczady and open-air museum season. Worth it for hiking, but crowded in Solina.</li><li><strong>September–October</strong> — second best. Autumn colours, fewer people, mushroom picking in the forests.</li><li><strong>November–March</strong> — the city is sleepy in winter, but museums are open. Worth it for those seeking silence.</li></ul><h2>Getting there</h2><ul><li><strong>By car:</strong> ~1.5 hours from Rzeszów (DK28), ~3.5 hours from Kraków (DK28), ~5.5 hours from Warsaw (S7 + DK28).</li><li><strong>By train:</strong> Sanok has a railway station, with connections from Kraków and Rzeszów.</li><li><strong>By bus:</strong> PKS serves Sanok, Lesko, Ustrzyki Dolne, Solina.</li></ul><p>Sanok is best explored on foot — the city is small, and attractions are close together. The Bieszczady is best explored by car or on foot.</p><h2>How much time you need</h2><p><strong>A weekend (2 days)</strong> is the minimum — Market Square, Castle, Open-air museum.</p><p><strong>Three days</strong> is realistic for a first visit. Market Square, Castle, Open-air museum, Historical Museum, Bieszczady.</p><p><strong>A week</strong> lets you explore all of Sanok and the Bieszczady — from Lesko to Ustrzyki Dolne.</p><h2>What to eat and where to sleep</h2><ul><li><strong>Pierogi with bryndza</strong> — regional pierogi with sheep cheese, available at inns.</li><li><strong>Proziaki</strong> — flatbreads of flour and sour milk, served with butter or lard.</li><li><strong>Trout from the San</strong> — from local farms, available at inns.</li><li><strong>Goat cheeses</strong> — from local farms, available in village shops.</li><li><strong>Beer from Subcarpathian breweries</strong> — several craft breweries in the region.</li></ul><p>Accommodation: Sanok (city, more options), Lesko (small town, guesthouses), Solina (village, guesthouses), Ustrzyki Dolne (small town, guesthouses).</p><h2>Practical tips</h2><ul><li><strong>Guided tours</strong> — Sanok's history is rich, take a guide at the castle and open-air museum.</li><li><strong>Icons</strong> — the Historical Museum has about 700 icons. Allow at least 2 hours for them.</li><li><strong>Shopping</strong> — buy honey, cheese, and cold cuts directly from farmers.</li><li><strong>Open-air museum</strong> — in season, craft demonstrations and folklore events take place. Check the calendar.</li><li><strong>Weather</strong> — Bieszczady weather can be changeable, even in July bring a jacket.</li><li><strong>Signal</strong> — good in the city, weaker in the Bieszczady. Have an offline map.</li></ul>",
  "tip": "Visit the Beksiński studio at the Historical Museum — one of the most important collections of this painter in Poland, with his paintings and photographs.",
  "gallery": ["sanok.webp", "sanok1.webp", "sanok2.webp"]
